<p>Mandan is associated with four of the 31 people who had served as North Dakota governor when this article was written: one was born in the city, one died there, one moved there, and one began his professional career there.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/biographies/william-wild-bill-langer/274-langer-portrait-desk.gif" alt="William Langer seated at his desk">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>William Langer was born September 30, 1886, on a farm near Casselton to Frank and Mary Weber Langer. One of six children, he grew up in a family involved in county and state politics. His father was also an officer of Casselton's First National Bank.</p>

<p>Langer attended rural schools and graduated as Casselton High School's valedictorian in 1904. He received a bachelor of laws from the University of North Dakota in 1906 and passed the bar examination.</p>

<p>At 18, Langer was too young to practice law in North Dakota, where the minimum age was 21. He continued his education at Columbia University in New York and graduated at the top of his class in 1910. Although offered a position at a prominent New York firm, he returned to North Dakota.</p>

<p>Langer lived at a Mandan boardinghouse at 300 Second Avenue Northwest and worked for Morton County State's Attorney H. R. Bitzing. He served as assistant state's attorney until 1914, when he became state's attorney. On his first day, he issued 167 arrest warrants for liquor dealers and vice operators. He successfully sued the Northern Pacific Railway for $1.25 million in back taxes and gained a reputation as an opponent of corporations.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
