    <figcaption>Morton County Courthouse, circa 1913</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Langer and Mandan attorney S. L. Nuchols formed Langer and Nuchols in 1915. Langer was elected North Dakota attorney general in 1916, defeating his opponent by more than 58,000 votes and carrying every county. He won reelection in 1918 on the Nonpartisan League ticket.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/biographies/william-wild-bill-langer/237-william-and-lydia-langer-voting-c-1940.gif" alt="William and Lydia Langer voting around 1940">
    <figcaption>William and Lydia Langer voting, circa 1940</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Langer married Lydia Cady in New York City on February 26, 1918, while serving as attorney general. They had met while he attended Columbia. Langer recalled first noticing Lydia at a concert, arranging a false telephone call to draw her escort away, and introducing himself. The couple had four daughters: Emma, Lydia, Mary, and Cornelia.</p>

<p>In March 1920, Langer sought the governorship as a Progressive Republican. He had left the Nonpartisan League in 1919, accusing its leaders of selling out farmers, and narrowly lost to league candidate Lynn Frazier. Langer returned to law practice, moved the firm to Bismarck, and benefited from his statewide recognition.</p>

<p>He later rejoined the league and helped address its financial troubles. After losing the 1928 attorney-general primary, he secured the 1932 gubernatorial nomination. Langer and the other major league candidates swept that year's election.</p>

<p>As governor, Langer strongly supported farmers. When wheat prices fell, he imposed an embargo on North Dakota wheat until prices improved. He also declared a moratorium on farm foreclosures and used the National Guard to halt sheriff's sales.</p>

<p>Langer soon encountered legal trouble. He removed many state officials, appointed loyalists, and solicited state employees to subscribe to his newspaper at a cost of about 5 percent of their annual salary. Langer regarded the subscriptions as legitimate campaign fundraising, but a federal court convicted him of illegally soliciting contributions, sentenced him to 18 months in prison, and fined him $10,000. The North Dakota Supreme Court removed him from office because of the felony conviction. After three more trials, Langer was acquitted of all charges.</p>

<p>Langer returned as governor from 1937 through 1941 and then won election to the U.S. Senate. Opponents petitioned to prevent him from taking his seat. A Senate committee heard damaging testimony, including Langer's admission that he had paid the son of the judge who presided over two of his 1935 trials. The committee voted 13–3 against seating him, but the full Senate rejected its recommendation by 52–30. The decision made national headlines.</p>

