    <figcaption>Federal rural-electrification poster, circa 1934</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>In the Senate, Langer served on the Civil Service, Indian Affairs, and Judiciary committees, among others. He advocated rural electricity and telephone service and affordable health care. A strict isolationist, he opposed the Lend-Lease Act and extending Selective Service before World War II, but voted for war after Japan attacked Pearl Harbor. His isolationism continued after the war.</p>

<p>Langer was sharply critical of Winston Churchill. Before one visit by the former British prime minister, Langer telegraphed the pastor of Boston's Old North Church and asked that two lanterns be placed in its belfry to warn that the British were coming.</p>

<p>Langer won reelection in 1946 and 1952. Despite poor health for both him and Lydia and without making a campaign appearance in North Dakota, he carried every county in 1958. Langer died in Washington on November 8, 1959, while serving in the Senate. He is buried in St. Leo's Catholic Cemetery in Casselton.</p>
